messages.content:
{
  "message": "Since Wi‑Fi is ON and other devices connect fine, the fastest next step is to try the other band first, then power‑cycle the router if needed.",
  "thought_process": "Trying the other band (2.4 GHz vs 5 GHz) is quick and often works if the phone and router prefer different bands or if the signal on one band is weak. If that doesn’t work, a router power‑cycle refreshes its Wi‑Fi radios and DHCP, which can resolve stubborn handshake issues.",
  "recommended_order": [
    "1) Try connecting to the other band now.",
    "2) If that fails, power‑cycle the router and try again.",
    "3) If still not connecting, test a guest network or another known-good Wi‑Fi to isolate phone vs. router.",
    "4) If it connects elsewhere but not to your home SSID, it’s likely a router-side configuration issue. If it fails everywhere, we’ll do deeper device checks."
  ],
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Connect to the other band",
      "actions": [
        "Forget the current SSID (if it shows as Saved).",
        "Select your other band SSID (e.g., MyHome‑2G if you were trying MyHome‑5G, or vice versa).",
        "Enter the password exactly (case‑sensitive).",
        "Run check_wifi_status and confirm whether it shows Connected to [SSID]."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "If still not connecting, power‑cycle the router",
      "actions": [
        "Unplug the router’s power for 30 seconds.",
        "Plug it back in and wait 2 full minutes for Wi‑Fi to broadcast.",
        "On your phone, toggle Wi‑Fi OFF then ON, select your SSID again, and re‑enter the password.",
        "Run check_wifi_status and see if it shows Connected to [SSID]."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Isolate phone vs. router",
      "actions": [
        "Try connecting your phone to a different Wi‑Fi (e.g., guest network on your router, a work/cafe network, or a personal hotspot from another phone).",
        "If it connects elsewhere, your phone’s Wi‑Fi is fine and the home router likely needs a setting tweak.",
        "If it fails everywhere, it points to a device-side issue and we’ll proceed with additional checks."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "optional_advanced": [
    {
      "title": "If you’re on iPhone",
      "actions": [
        "Settings > Wi‑Fi > tap the “i” next to your SSID > toggle Private Wi‑Fi Address OFF (as a test), then try joining again.",
        "If it still fails, Settings > General > Transfer or Reset > Reset > Reset Network Settings (this forgets Wi‑Fi networks and Bluetooth devices). Then try joining your SSID again."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "If you’re on Android",
      "actions": [
        "Settings > Wi‑Fi > your SSID > Forget. Then Advanced/Privacy > MAC address type: set to Phone/Device MAC (not Randomized) as a test, and try joining again.",
        "If it still fails, you can reset network settings (location varies by manufacturer: Settings > System > Reset options > Reset Wi‑Fi, mobile & Bluetooth). Then try joining your SSID again."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "questions": [
    "After trying the other band, did it connect? If not, what exact message (Incorrect password, Authentication error, Saved, or just not connecting)?",
    "What does check_wifi_status show after the router power‑cycle and rejoin attempt?",
    "Are you on iPhone or Android? I can tailor the advanced steps precisely."
  ],
  "next": "Please try the other band first and let me know the result. If it doesn’t connect, proceed with the router power‑cycle and share the updated check_wifi_status output."
}

